# C, THE COUPLING INCIDENCE, PROJECTED TO WHAT AN `association` CAN CARRY. One row per
# (filing, from_layer, to_layer): this filing observed that relieving one layer moves the other.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ THE MODEL'S OWN FALSIFIER, DRAWN. `pm:Coupling` exists *so that the model can be REFUTED
#    IN ITS OWN FORMAT*: a stack is assumed to be independent quantizations, and a coupling is
#    the observation that says it is not. A diagram silent about it is the artifact a reader is
#    most likely to be shown AND the one incapable of carrying the refutation, which is what
#    this relation is here to prevent.
#
# ⭐⭐ IT IS DIRECTED, AND THAT WAS CHECKED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. `composition/attenuated.sqlc`
#    matches from to from and to to to, `entries/spillovers.sqlc` joins each end separately, and
#    `checks/coupling_does_not_attenuate.sqlc` formats its own detail as `%s->%s`. ⚠️ The
#    SYMMETRIC falsifier is a different relation: `checks/layers_move_together.sqlc` reads
#    `rank/co_moving_layers.sqlc`, which is the descent intersected with its converse, so it is
#    about F and never about C. Two rules, two graphs, one English phrase.
#
# ⛔⛔ INCIDENCE ONLY, AND HERE THAT REFUSES THE `observation` AS WELL AS THE STRENGTH. The
#   observation is REQUIRED prose and it is the whole evidence, and two of the five in this corpus
#   contain a numeral: one quotes `[0.10, 0.22, 0.35]` outright. Carrying it into a
#   `documentation` would put a magnitude on a page that has no unit anywhere, which is exactly
#   the refusal `dataObject` carries, arriving through prose instead of through a field.
#
# ⚠️ SO THE ARTIFACT SAYS THAT TWO LAYERS ARE COUPLED AND CANNOT SAY WHAT WAS SEEN. That is a
#   `loses` and it is a wide one. It is still the first time the fact appears at all.
-- pm:Stack/pm:Coupling projected to its two ends; BPMN 2.0 tAssociation sourceRef/targetRef.
SELECT c.filing, c.from_layer, c.to_layer
FROM (
    :compose(entries/couplings.sqlc)
) c
